WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:11.360
Today, we are going to absolutely destroy&nbsp;
this virtual bunny. And then, inflate this cat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:18.080
until it becomes… a bit of a chonker. And, I hope&nbsp;
you like dumplings, because we’ll also be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:26.160
start a cooking show in a virtual world. For&nbsp;
instance, we can now squash and wrap and pinch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.840
and squeeze. And this is a computer&nbsp;
graphics paper, so let’s smash these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.040
things for good measure. There we go. Add&nbsp;
some more. And the meal is now ready. Enjoy!

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.680
This is all possible through this new paper&nbsp;
that is capable of creating physics simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.240
with drastic geometry changes. And when I say&nbsp;
drastic, I really mean it. And looking through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.400
the results, this paper feels like it can do&nbsp;
absolutely anything. It promises a technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:06.320
called Injective deformation processing. What&nbsp;
does all this mean? It means great news. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:12.240
when we do these crazy experiments, things&nbsp;
don’t turn inside out, and the geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.840
does not overlap. Wanna see what these&nbsp;
overlaps looks like? Well, here it is.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.400
And, luckily, we don’t have to worry about&nbsp;
this phenomenon with this new technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:29.840
Not only when inflating, but, it also works&nbsp;
correctly when we start deflating things. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.920
talking about overlaps, let’s see this animation&nbsp;
sequence simulated with a previous method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.360
Oh no! The belly is not moving,&nbsp;
and, my goodness. Look at that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.000
It gets even worse. What is even&nbsp;
worse than a belly that does not move?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.840
Of course, intersection artifacts. Now, what you&nbsp;
will see is not a re-simulation of this experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.600
from scratch with this with the new method,&nbsp;
no-no. Even better. We give this flawed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:10.000
simulation to the new technique, and…yes, it&nbsp;
can even repair it! Wow. An absolute miracle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.640
No more self-intersections, and finally, the&nbsp;
belly is moving around in a realistic manner.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.880
And, while we find out whether this armadillo&nbsp;
simulated with the new method is dabbing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.880
or if it is just shy, let’s talk about how long&nbsp;
we have to wait for a simulation like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:36.560
All nighters? No, not at all! The font inflation&nbsp;
examples take roughly half a second per frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.200
that is unbelievable, and it goes up to 12 minutes&nbsp;
per frame, which is required for the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.480
deformation experiments. Repairing an&nbsp;
already existing, flawed simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.480
also takes a similar amount of time.
So, what about this armadillo? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.120
that is definitely a shy armadillo. So, from&nbsp;
now on, we can apply drastic geometry changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.880
in our virtual worlds, and I am sure that two more&nbsp;
papers down the line, all this will be possible in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:13.920
real time. Real-time dumplings in a virtual world?&nbsp;
Yes please, sign me up! What a time to be alive!

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

